that actually makes your software scale despite using agents.

5
00:00:27,534 --> 00:00:36,160
So first of all, what actually is a skill in the world of agentic development? A skill is in the end really just a markdown file after all.

6
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:41,680
A markdown file that contains contents on how to do something.

7
00:00:41,966 --> 00:00:50,320
So of course, when you're developing software with clock code, codecs, anti-gravity, etc., then very often you have certain repetitive tasks.

8
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:55,520
You have certain instructions that you always want to give your agent in specific scenarios.

9
00:00:55,726 --> 00:01:01,200
So instructions that aren't always applicable, but instructions that are often enough applicable.

10
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:05,280
For example, how your agent should approach writing test cases.

11
00:01:05,486 --> 00:01:13,360
So you maybe have your own conventions for how test cases are written in your project, what kinds of libraries should be chosen there, how assertions should be implemented,

12
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:19,120
how fine-green the test cases should actually be, some maybe best practices you you have for yourself.

13
00:01:19,438 --> 00:01:25,988
So a lot of these instructions you would actually have to tell the agent over and over again about without skills.

14
00:01:26,126 --> 00:01:34,400
That is one thing. And the other thing is you would also have to kind of tell the agent about that when it's already about to write a test case. But that's something you often don't know.

15
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:43,840
Maybe you're just giving it a larger task and then it starts writing some test cases. But that's actually the point of time where you want to feed in this context on how to write those test cases.

16
00:01:44,014 --> 00:01:46,400
And that's exactly what a skill solves.

17
00:01:46,606 --> 00:01:50,560
So here I've opened such a skill for myself, for example, for Android unit testing.

18
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:56,420
And by the way, if you're into Android development, you can download my top eight skills for Android Development for completely free,

19
00:01:56,814 --> 00:02:02,480
clicking the link in this video's description for architecture, unit testing, building UI components, etc. Like all of that.

20
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:06,660
I've really generated skills and iterated on these a lot, which you can download for free.

21
00:02:06,926 --> 00:02:14,000
But just to give you an idea of what such a skill consists of, this one here would be for writing unit tests for an Android application.

22
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:19,652
And here we can really outline in a lot of detail what writing good unit tests really means.

23
00:02:19,982 --> 00:02:25,760
And in my case, I've taught it that the core methodology is really to test as someone who really wants to break the code.

24
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:36,080
Because I found that normally when just telling Claude to write test cases itself, without any explicit instructions on how to do that, it always kind of undertested

25
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:39,520
the behavior and didn't think of a lot of edge cases or so.

26
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:43,920
But if we have this methodology of saying, okay, how can we break this code?

27
00:02:44,142 --> 00:02:47,076
How can we write a test case that will probably break this code?

28
00:02:47,790 --> 00:02:52,900
That is, of course, a much more solid approach, and you will get a lot more test cases that way from your agent.

29
00:02:53,486 --> 00:03:02,240
And this is like, in the end, exactly how how this skill is like. You really just have instructions. It's nothing else than a prompt that, however, can be loaded on demand.

30
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:03,520
That's the big thing here.

31
00:03:03,790 --> 00:03:10,180
Cloud will now load the skill when it thinks it's appropriate, specifically when it's about to write a test case.

32
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:18,500
And then all of that content, when it thinks that's appropriate, will be loaded into Cloud's memory or your agent's memory after all.

33
00:03:18,798 --> 00:03:21,188
And it knows exactly on how to really do that.

34
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:29,604
here in your skill MD, so it's really just a markdown file, and this is like a standardized format for all sorts of skills across agentic coding tools.

35
00:03:30,318 --> 00:03:33,572
This like a skill on the one that has a name, but it also has a description.

36
00:03:34,126 --> 00:03:39,264
And this description is in the end what is always part of your context window.

37
00:03:39,502 --> 00:03:47,716
So if you have 10 skills, for example, and you make a prompt, then all the descriptions from all these skills will be part of your context window, will occupy tokens.

38
00:03:47,982 --> 00:03:55,524
So that also means you shouldn't flood your agent with skills because they always still take some context because this description is after all

39
00:03:55,904 --> 00:03:59,584
what your agent then uses to know whether it should invoke the skill or not.

40
00:03:59,758 --> 00:04:04,484
So there are scenarios where a skill is not the right tool and where a skill is the right tool.

41
00:04:04,718 --> 00:04:05,424
What are those?

42
00:04:05,614 --> 00:04:14,544
On the one hand, what you always want to make sure before creating a skill, you want to make sure that the instructions that the skill encodes are needed repetitively.

43
00:04:14,704 --> 00:04:22,384
If you want your agent to do something you just need once, there is no value in creating a skill because all of the content you would in that case just normally prompt into your agent.

44
00:04:22,510 --> 00:04:28,100
But something like unit testing for Android development is of course something that I will need over and over again, even across code bases.

45
00:04:28,398 --> 00:04:33,024
So we can spend the time once to define these very detailed requirements for how unit testing works.

46
00:04:33,230 --> 00:04:35,344
And from this point on we can really profit from that.

47
00:04:35,630 --> 00:04:38,052
So that's criteria number one that has to be fulfilled.

48
00:04:38,414 --> 00:04:43,664
And then another criteria out of two optional criteria has to be fulfilled. So at least one of these.

49
00:04:43,886 --> 00:04:49,184
And that is on the one hand that you're working with very niche or domain knowledge you want to encode in the skill.

50
00:04:49,344 --> 00:04:51,504
And it's not about general purpose knowledge.

51
00:04:51,664 --> 00:05:00,132
So for example, creating a skill that helps you design a relational database schema. This is probably not a good idea to create that.

52
00:05:00,334 --> 00:05:05,664
Because creating relational databases, there aren't many very different or unique approaches.

53
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:10,624
There are just some general best practices, things, how we should do that, how we should approach that.

54
00:05:10,830 --> 00:05:14,624
But this knowledge about that is genuinely available. It's publicly available.

55
00:05:14,734 --> 00:05:19,264
And your normal agent, your normal LLM already knows how to do that well.

56
00:05:19,502 --> 00:05:30,308
Unless you, of course, say you have a very specific custom approach of doing that, or you have very specific requirements that your agent can't know from this general public training data.

57
00:05:30,734 --> 00:05:34,384
If that's not the case, it doesn't make sense to really create a skill in this case.

58
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:39,424
Over here, for example, for Android unit testing, I have my very own conventions, I have my very own way of doing that.

59
00:05:39,504 --> 00:05:47,012
But what could of course also be the case is that you just have some niche domain knowledge, because of course LLMs are trained on the general public of training data,

60
00:05:47,246 --> 00:05:56,624
but maybe you're working in a very niche domain, I don't know, designing some kind of electric circuits for a specific type of machine or so;

61
00:05:56,864 --> 00:06:02,544
then chances are you will know more about these circuits, about this machine, than Claude knows from its training data.

62
00:06:02,704 --> 00:06:08,784
So here you could actually encode your own domain knowledge in such a skill to make sure Claude will also know this or whatever agent you use.

63
00:06:09,006 --> 00:06:16,624
So this must be fulfilled along with the skill being needed repetitively, or alternatively, it's about specific conventions to follow.

64
00:06:16,784 --> 00:06:20,100
And that's indeed what I mentioned here with this unit testing skill.

65
00:06:20,304 --> 00:06:28,704
This is not about specific niche knowledge, something that isn't available elsewhere, but it's about specific conventions that I just prefer,

